(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What happens during the inflammatory phase of wound healing, and over what period?","choices":["Days 1 to 3: type I collagen is laid down in basket-weave orientation","Days 1 to 3: a fibrin haemostatic plug forms, neutrophils and macrophages remove dead tissue, and growth factors and cytokines are secreted to signal the next phase","Days 4 to 21: granulation tissue forms, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells that rebuild the wound bed from below over three weeks","Days 21 to one year: type III collagen is replaced by type I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The phase both cleans the wound and issues the signal that starts proliferation. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which vascular source accounts for anterior epistaxis, and what proportion of cases is that?","choices":["The anterior ethmoidal artery, about 10 per cent","Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall, about 90 per cent","Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum, about 90 per cent","The sphenopalatine artery, about 50 per cent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nine in ten nosebleeds are anterior and come from this plexus, which is why direct compression works so often. The posterior 10 per cent come from Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the symptom set of acute angle-closure glaucoma?","choices":["Severe eye pain, headache, halos around lights, cloudy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated pupil, and nausea and vomiting","Painless gradual bilateral blurring with glare at night","Flashing lights, a sudden shower of floaters, and a curtain falling across the field","Gradual central distortion with a scotoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the full acute picture. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Ulcerative inflammation is characterised by which change?","choices":["Formation of a membrane-like covering over intact and otherwise viable mucosa","Damage to blood vessels occurring with other exudates","Coagulative necrosis of an entire limb segment","Necrosis and sloughing of surface epithelium, exposing underlying connective tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That exposure of the underlying connective tissue is what defines the ulcerative pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where does the gene damage in carcinogenesis come from?","choices":["It is always acquired during life and never inherited","It arises only from errors during deoxyribonucleic acid replication","Inherited, and/or acquired from chemicals, radiation or microbes","It is always inherited through the germline and never acquired during life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the named sources are chemical carcinogens, ultraviolet and ionizing radiation, and microbial organisms: viruses and one bacterium. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the tissue basis of a papule?","choices":["Focal loss of the epidermis that does not penetrate below the dermal-epidermal junction beneath it","Hyperplasia of the epidermis with hyperkeratosis, plus inflammation and dense collagen fibres in the dermis","Fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or beneath the epidermis","Separation of epidermis from dermis with fluid accumulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and seborrhoeic keratosis is the example given. A papule is elevated, solid, and under 5 millimetres. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Digestion of foreign material by activated neutrophils and macrophages involves which four steps?","choices":["Vasodilation, exudation, stasis, and repair","Adhesion, transmigration, aggregation, and chemotaxis","Recognition, attachment, engulfment, and degradation","Opsonisation, complement fixation, lysis, and clearance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those four, and recognition and attachment are enhanced when opsonins are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as PERIPHERAL rather than central?","choices":["Absence of nystagmus entirely","Gradual onset, vertical nystagmus, non-suppressible, with neurological deficits","Gradual onset with hearing loss and tinnitus","Sudden onset, horizontal or rotational nystagmus, fatigable, suppressed by visual fixation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four point at the inner ear or cranial nerve VIII. Suppression by visual fixation is the one most easily tested at the bedside. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What effects does serotonin have?","choices":["Pain generation and leukocyte chemotaxis","Opsonisation of biomaterials","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Formation of a fibrous network trapping microorganisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serotonin is given the same pair of vascular effects as histamine, which is why the two are grouped together. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A benign tumour arises from cartilage. Which category does it fall into?","choices":["Epithelial, since cartilage lines a surface","Glandular, so it would be called an adenoma","Neither, since the classification covers only epithelial tumours","Mesenchymal, since cartilage is supportive tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — connective tissue, adipose, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone are all listed. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do hypertrophic scars and keloids depart from normal healing?","choices":["Type I collagen is replaced by weaker type III in basket-weave orientation","Fibroblast dysregulation prolongs the proliferative phase, collagen deposition and degradation become imbalanced during remodelling, and collagen bundles develop haphazardly and exceed the boundaries of the initial wound","Keratinocyte division slows and elastin degrades, thinning the tissue","The inflammatory phase never resolves, so granulation tissue never forms at all and the wound remains open indefinitely without ever progressing to the proliferative or remodelling phases that normally follow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two phases go wrong, and exceeding the original wound margin is the defining outcome. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What happens to collagen during the remodelling phase, and how does the result differ from normal dermis?","choices":["Collagen production continues to increase indefinitely","Type III collagen is replaced with stronger type I collagen oriented in small parallel bundles, whereas normal dermis has a basket-weave orientation","Type I collagen is replaced with weaker type III collagen oriented in a basket-weave pattern throughout the scar tissue that results","Collagen is degraded entirely and replaced by elastin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The scar is strong but architecturally different, which is why it never quite matches the surrounding skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which lid deformity goes with dacryoadenitis?","choices":["An outward-turning lid margin","A drooping upper lid from levator weakness","An S-shaped lid deformity","An inward-turning lid margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the characteristic sign of lacrimal gland inflammation. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What makes the eye the only place in the body where live neural tissue can be seen directly?","choices":["The cornea contains myelinated tracts that can be seen on slit lamp","The sclera is thin enough to transilluminate the whole globe","The optic nerve head is visible through the pupil without cutting any tissue","The retina can be biopsied more safely than any other neural tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the eye is a nervous window for exactly this reason. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the steps of haematogenous spread, in order?","choices":["Cells separate and degrade intercellular tissue with enzymes, invade the vessel, then travel","Tumour cells adhere more tightly to each other; the mass compresses a vessel; a single fragment travels to one other organ","Tumour cells enter lymphatic vessels at the margin and follow the natural route of drainage","Tumour invades through an organ surface into a body cavity and settles on the serosal membranes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — multiple tumour fragments travel to other organs, so one organ may end up with several metastatic nodules. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What can a hyperopic patient see well?","choices":["Objects far away","Objects up close","Neither near nor far without correction","Only objects directly in the central field"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hyperopia is farsightedness, so distance vision is the preserved one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the main pathologic finding in nail clubbing, and what mediates it?","choices":["A halt of keratin production at the matrix","Incomplete keratinization at the nail bed","Increased capillary density, with increased release of vascular endothelial growth factor","Overgrowth of the connective tissue within the nail bed itself over a period of time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is associated with lung, cardiovascular, liver and inflammatory bowel disease. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What does falling differentiation mean for the behaviour of a tumour?","choices":["Aggressiveness is unrelated to differentiation and depends only on stage","It becomes more aggressive only once the basement membrane has been breached","It becomes more aggressive","It becomes less aggressive, because poorly differentiated cells divide more slowly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an anaplastic carcinoma is very aggressive compared with a well differentiated one. Grading is a scale of resemblance, and prognosis tracks it. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 24:08"},{"q":"How is episcleritis best characterised?","choices":["A vision-threatening emergency with severe deep boring pain and scleral melting","An infection of the corneal stroma","A granulomatous blockage of a meibomian gland","A self-limiting inflammatory blush, not vision-threatening and without severe pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — in deliberate contrast to scleritis. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What can an adenoma sometimes do that reflects its origin?","choices":["Produce finger-like projections from the epithelial surface","Secrete the hormone or hormones produced by its gland of origin","Invade through the basement membrane into surrounding tissue","Seed the peritoneal cavity through an organ surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the tumour retains a function of the tissue it came from. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is a malignant tumour of mesenchymal origin called?","choices":["An adenoma","A papilloma","A sarcoma","A carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal means supportive tissue, and the malignant form is the sarcoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which tumours are associated with an inherited alteration of the retinoblastoma protein?","choices":["Retinoblastoma, a rare childhood tumour of the eye, and osteosarcoma","Neurofibromatosis types 1 and 2, with tumours of the central and peripheral nervous system","Malignant melanoma","Familial adenomatous polyposis and colon cancer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same tumour suppressor, two very different tumours. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the pathophysiology of actinic keratosis, and what does it carry a higher risk of?","choices":["Benign proliferation of immature keratinocytes, carrying a higher risk for basal cell carcinoma of the head and neck region in later life especially","Human papillomavirus infection of basal cells, carrying higher risk for melanoma","Autoimmune T cell infiltration, carrying higher risk for lymphoma","Damage from excessive cumulative ultraviolet exposure leading to intraepidermal proliferation of dysplastic keratinocytes, carrying higher risk for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is described as the most common precancer, which is exactly why the dysplasia matters. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Why does stasis of blood flow matter mechanistically in acute inflammation?","choices":["It terminates the inflammatory response","It precedes leukocyte adhesion in the sequence, as slowed flow allows leukocytes to contact the endothelium","It marks the point at which acute inflammation gives way to chronic inflammation in the tissue","It reverses the earlier increase in permeability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Haemoconcentration and stasis sit immediately before leukocyte adhesion in the reviewed sequence. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the pattern of onset in presbycusis?","choices":["Gradual, symmetrical and bilateral","Sudden and unilateral","Episodic and fluctuating","Gradual but strictly unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is sensorineural. Asymmetry would point away from ageing toward a focal lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does an ulcer scar while an erosion generally does not?","choices":["An ulcer involves a loss of melanocytes from the basal layer which cannot be reversed once it has occurred in the tissue at any point afterwards","An ulcer destroys collagen in the dermis, so healing must lay down new collagen; an erosion spares the dermal-epidermal junction and the dermis beneath it","An ulcer is infected and an erosion is sterile","An ulcer is larger, and size alone determines scarring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Scarring is a dermal repair process, so a lesion confined above the junction has nothing to scar. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the three causes of chronic inflammation?","choices":["Trauma, thermal injury, and radiation exposure","Persistent infection, prolonged exposure to potentially toxic agents, and autoimmunity","Vasodilation, increased permeability, and exudation","Opsonisation of foreign material, phagocytosis by neutrophils, and enzymatic degradation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the three given, with rheumatoid arthritis and lupus offered as the autoimmune examples. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which vascular change is directly responsible for allowing fluid and blood proteins to enter the interstitium?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the draining venules","Endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability","Haemoconcentration occurring within the capillary bed","Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endothelial contraction is what allows the increased permeability through which exudation occurs. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Besides invasion into adjacent structures, which abnormal interaction is a step in invasion?","choices":["Abnormal apoptosis of neighbouring stromal cells","Abnormal lymphatic valve function","Abnormal cell-substratum interaction","Abnormal cell-cycle checkpoint control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the cell's relationship with its own supporting matrix changes. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What defines vertigo, and what distinguishes it from dizziness?","choices":["A hallucination of motion, with NO syncope","A sensation of impending faint from cerebral hypoperfusion","Any unsteadiness on standing","Loss of consciousness preceded by spinning"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry between the two sides is the mechanism, and the absence of syncope is the separator. Dizziness or lightheadedness is a non-vestibular sensation of impending faint. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What makes the middle ear vulnerable to the pressure changes behind otitis media?","choices":["It is an air-filled space vented only by the Eustachian tube","It has no blood supply","It is lined by keratinised skin","It is fluid-filled and cannot compress"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A sealed air pocket with one vent is why closing the vent drops the pressure and draws in fluid. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which chemical carcinogens are classically emphasised in occupational bladder cancer?","choices":["Aromatic amines","Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons","Alkylating agents","Asbestos fibres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons come from tobacco combustion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What tissue makes up a scale?","choices":["Dried sebum, cellular debris, blood or necrotic skin","Thickened epidermis induced by long-term scratching","Focal loss of epidermis and dermis with destruction of the collagen beneath","A compact portion of desquamating stratum corneum, varying in size and thickness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and psoriasis is the example given — which follows from its epidermal hyperplasia and loss of the granular layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Above what cup-to-disc ratio is optic disc cupping progressive?","choices":["Greater than 0.8","Greater than 1.0","Greater than 0.5","Greater than 0.2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is the threshold for an increased cup-to-disc ratio. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of wet, neovascular macular degeneration?","choices":["Extracellular lipofuscin debris accumulating beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Fibrovascular membranes on the retinal surface exert traction","Ganglion cell axons undergo apoptosis from raised pressure","Hypoxia and inflammation trigger choroidal neovascularisation, and new vessels breach beneath the pigment epithelium into the subretinal space"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — from trigger through to breach. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which skin cancer is described as the most common malignancy in humans, and how does it behave?","choices":["Actinic keratosis, which is by far the most common malignancy occurring in human beings","Basal cell carcinoma, slow growing and rarely metastasising, typically on the head and neck","Squamous cell carcinoma, rapidly metastasising","Melanoma, slow growing and rarely metastasising"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What single feature separates a pterygium from a pinguecula?","choices":["The pterygium encroaches onto the cornea; the pinguecula does not","The pterygium is yellow; the pinguecula is triangular","The pterygium is painful; the pinguecula is painless","The pterygium is bilateral; the pinguecula is unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Webster put it as crossing the limbus, and called this section important. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does a lesion of cranial nerve VIII produce the same audiometric pattern as cochlear damage?","choices":["Both add mass to the tympanic membrane","Both stiffen the ossicles","Both obstruct the canal","Both sit beyond the conducting chain, so both give sensorineural loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The category is defined by where the defect sits, not by which of the two structures failed. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does human papillomavirus produce a common wart?","choices":["It triggers mast cell degranulation in the dermis","It digests keratin using keratinases and proteases","It invades epidermal basal cells through microabrasions, leading to proliferation of the epidermis","It infects the melanocytes that lie along the dermal-epidermal junction within the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and clinical variants of warts are often associated with distinct viral subtypes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"How does dry macular degeneration affect vision?","choices":["Rapid central vision loss over days","Loss of peripheral vision first, with the central field spared until late","Sudden painless monocular blindness","Slow gradual loss of central detail, with distortion and a scotoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — metamorphopsia and scotoma, developing slowly. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How do the causes of anterior and posterior epistaxis differ?","choices":["Anterior from hypertension and anticoagulation; posterior from digital trauma","Both are caused predominantly by anticoagulation","Anterior from coagulopathy; posterior from low humidity","Anterior from local trauma and dry air; posterior from hypertension and anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anterior bleeds are local and mechanical; posterior bleeds reflect a systemic vascular or clotting problem, which is also why they are more profuse and carry airway risk. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which hair type is regulated by androgens, and which grows independently of them?","choices":["Lanugo hair is androgen-regulated; terminal hair is not","All three types are equally androgen-dependent","Terminal hair is androgen-regulated; vellus hair grows independently of androgens","Vellus hair is regulated by androgens; terminal hair grows independently of them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lanugo is the third type, the fine hair of newborns. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the natural course of a subconjunctival haemorrhage?","choices":["Benign, resolving over one to two weeks with bruise-like colour changes","Progressive, threatening vision within days","Permanent, leaving a fibrovascular scar","Recurrent by definition, so it requires long-term ophthalmology follow-up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — benign, resolving over one to two weeks. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which way does the nystagmus of labyrinthitis beat?","choices":["Vertical and non-suppressible","Absent, because hearing rather than balance is affected","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating AWAY from the affected side","Horizontal-rotary, with the fast phase beating TOWARD the affected side"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The damaged side is under-firing, so the slow drift is toward it and the corrective fast phase beats away. Severe postural instability, gait ataxia and nausea come with it. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the characteristic frequency pattern of presbycusis, and why does it impair conversation?","choices":["High frequencies go first, taking the speech consonants","Low frequencies go first, taking the vowels","All frequencies fall equally","Only frequencies above the speech range are affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Consonants such as /s/, /f/ and /t/ are high-frequency sounds and carry most of the discriminating information in speech, which is why voices become hard to make out in a noisy room while still being audible. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What proportion of circulating serum proteins do complement components represent, and what are their listed actions?","choices":["25%; responsible for the formation of a clot at the site of the injury or lesion","10%; chemotactic to neutrophils and monocytes, with the cascade able to damage bacteria","50%; primarily responsible for oncotic pressure","1%; acting solely as opsonins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three points are given together. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is canalithiasis?","choices":["Dislodged otoconia floating free in the semicircular canals","Accumulation of endolymph in the membranous labyrinth","Inflammatory debris within the cochlea after infection","Calcification of the stapes footplate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The crystals normally sit in the utricle. Once loose in the endolymph they strike structures and generate a false sensation of motion. Why they dislodge is not the examinable part &mdash; that they can is. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Roughly how many rods are there, and what are they for?","choices":["About 120 million, for dim light and peripheral vision","About 6 million, for dim light and peripheral vision","About 120 million, for colour vision and sharp acuity","About 20 million, for colour vision in bright light"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the count and the function for rods. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How do phagocytic cells recognise immunoglobulin G bound to foreign material?","choices":["Through histamine receptors on the endothelium","Through Fc receptors that recognise the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G","Through the complement receptors that bind to the C3b fragment","Through T cell receptors on the phagocyte surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages and neutrophils carry Fc receptors that bind the Fc portion of the antibody. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which pattern of inflammation is defined by mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane?","choices":["Catarrhal","Pseudomembranous","Serous","Haemorrhagic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catarrhal inflammation is mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes a vesicle from a bulla, mechanistically as well as by size?","choices":["A vesicle is solid and a bulla is fluid-filled","A vesicle contains purulent material and a bulla contains serous fluid","A vesicle is up to 5 millimetres with fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or directly beneath the epidermis; a bulla is greater than 5 millimetres and involves separation of epidermis from dermis","A vesicle is greater than 5 millimetres and a bulla is up to 5 millimetres in diameter, and the two share an identical mechanism of fluid accumulation entirely within the epidermis rather than beneath it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bulla represents a deeper plane of cleavage, not merely a bigger blister. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which patient group is at particular risk in keratitis, and from which organism?","choices":["Contact lens wearers, from Staphylococcus aureus","Elderly patients, from Streptococcus pneumoniae","Immunosuppressed patients, from Candida","Contact lens wearers, from Pseudomonas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — contact lens wearers, from Pseudomonas. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which combination of effects does bradykinin produce?","choices":["Increased capillary permeability, pain, and possibly increased leukocyte chemotaxis","Vasoconstriction, reduced capillary permeability, and analgesia at the site","Antibody production and antigen presentation","Coagulation and trapping of exudate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bradykinin is the plasma-protein mediator that adds pain to the permeability effect. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the classic example of dysplasia, and what usually causes it?","choices":["Barrett's oesophagus, usually caused by chronic acid reflux","Endometrial hyperplasia, usually caused by excess oestrogen","Skeletal muscle hypertrophy, usually caused by resistance training","Cervical dysplasia, usually caused by human papillomavirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — he added that it might become archival one day because of the vaccine. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 10:47"},{"q":"Which description matches the serous pattern of inflammation?","choices":["Mucus hypersecretion accompanying inflammation of a mucous membrane surface","Largely plasma with low protein content, occurring early or with mild inflammation","Large amounts of fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork","Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serous inflammation is largely plasma, low in protein, and occurs early or when inflammation is mild. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Where do the pressure waves that begin hearing actually travel first?","choices":["The cochlear duct itself","The scala vestibuli","The semicircular canals","The scala tympani"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stapes movement at the oval window starts them there, and they displace endolymph in the cochlear duct. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which two malignancies is Helicobacter pylori associated with?","choices":["Hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and Burkitt lymphoma","Cervical carcinoma and oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma","Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — usually abbreviated to MALT lymphoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why does the vertigo of Meniere disease come in attacks rather than continuously?","choices":["Inflammation resolves and recurs","Pressure builds until micro-ruptures occur, then re-equilibrates","The virus replicates in waves","Otoconia settle between episodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cycle of build-up and release is what makes it episodic. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The term agranulocyte is described as somewhat misleading. Why?","choices":["Because they are actually a subtype of polymorphonuclear leukocyte","Because these cells acquire granules only after activation","Because their granules are visible only in chronic inflammation","Because these cells do contain non-specific azurophilic granules — lysosomes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The name implies an absence of granules, but non-specific azurophilic granules (lysosomes) are present. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What do tumour suppressor genes do, and what are some examples?","choices":["They promote regulated cell growth; the examples are growth factors and their receptors","They repair damaged deoxyribonucleic acid; the examples are BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","They trigger apoptosis in damaged cells; no specific examples are named","They inhibit cell growth; the examples are NF-1, NF-2, retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — losing them removes a brake rather than adding an accelerator. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
